--TEST--
`&` in a by-reference position is `E0237` naming `inout`, an argument that binds an `inout` parameter without saying so is `E0713`, and one that says so where nothing binds it is `E0714` — `rule:statements/inout-is-the-by-reference-spelling`, `rule:statements/inout-is-written-at-the-call` and `rule:statements/ampersand-is-not-a-by-reference-marker`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:statements/ampersand-is-not-a-by-reference-marker` retires `&` as a by-reference marker outright, so every one of
// the five positions PHP writes it in takes the same code — including the two
// *returning* forms, which `inout` does not replace at all: a method hands
// back a value rather than a place, so there is nothing to mark.
class Old {
    public int $wrapped {
        &get => $this->wrapped * 2;
    }

    public function constructor(int $wrapped) {
        $this->wrapped = $wrapped;
    }

    public static function bump(int &$n): void {
        $n = $n + 1;
    }

    public static function &handOut(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

class Adder {
    public static function bump(inout int $n): void {
        $n = $n + 1;
    }

    public static function sum(int $a, int $b): int {
        return $a + $b;
    }
}

int $count = 5;
array<int> $rest = [1, 2];

// The other two binding positions, at file scope.
foreach ($rest as int &$v) {
    echo $v, "\n";
}
[int &$a] = $rest;

// § 2's call-site half, in both directions. An argument that binds an `inout`
// parameter and does not say so hides the write its callee makes; one that
// says so where the parameter is by value claims a write that never happens.
// The marker is deliberately not inference-assisted — a marker the compiler
// supplies is not a marker — so each is an error rather than a lint.
Adder::bump($count);
echo Adder::sum(inout $count, 2), "\n";

// Through a `callable` there is no parameter list to bind against at either
// end: `rule:types/callable-absorbs-closure` keeps the type opaque and refuses an `inout` closure
// parameter outright, so nothing this call can reach declares one.
callable $f = fn (int $n): int => $n + 1;
echo $f(inout $count), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0237]: `&` is not a by-reference marker in Novis
  --> case.nvs:8:9
